# Calculators and the spreadsheets, one stanza per program.

shot    loan
size    46 80
cap     An amortisation calculator.  It asks for the principal, the term
cap     in years, the yearly rate and the month payments start, and
cap     prints the monthly payment, the total interest, and a schedule
cap     of every month's principal, interest and balance, subtotalled
cap     at each year end.  Payments from August give a five-month first
cap     year.  The schedule runs the full term, so page it or cut it.
try     loan | head -n 40
os9     loan
run     loan | head -n 40
wait    3
send    120000\r
wait    2
send    30\r
wait    2
send    6.5\r
wait    2
send    8\r
wait    8

shot    cvtbase
cap     Converts numbers from one base to another.  The two arguments
cap     name the bases, in and out: d decimal, h or x hex, o octal, b
cap     binary, or the digit characters themselves for any other base.
cap     Each number typed is answered on the next line; Escape ends it.
try     cvtbase d h
run     cvtbase d h
wait    3
send    255\r
wait    2
send    4095\r
wait    2
send    \033
wait    2
run     cvtbase h b
wait    3
send    ff\r
wait    2
send    \033
wait    2

shot    rechne
cap     A German command-line calculator that gives every answer three
cap     ways: decimal, hex and binary.  The expression is one argument
cap     with no spaces; the operators are + - x / and m for modulo, a, o
cap     and p for and, or and xor, and $ prefixes a hex number.  -b lists
cap     which bits of the result are set.
try     rechne 4095+1
run     rechne 4095+1
wait    2
run     rechne 12x12
wait    2
run     rechne \$ff+1
wait    2
run     rechne 100m7
wait    2
run     rechne -b 12345
wait    3

shot    rpn
size    50 80
cap     A reverse-Polish calculator on whole numbers.  A number typed is
cap     pushed on the stack; the words add, sub, mul, div and mod combine
cap     the top two, and, or, xor and not work bitwise, pr prints an
cap     entry and pop discards one.  After each line it shows the top
cap     of the stack and how deep it is.  ? lists the words; q leaves.
try     rpn
run     rpn
wait    2
send    12\r
wait    1
send    34\r
wait    1
send    add\r
wait    1
send    100\r
wait    1
send    mul\r
wait    1
send    7\r
wait    1
send    div\r
wait    1
send    ?\r
wait    2
snap
send    q\r
wait    2

shot    checkfile
size    30 80
cap     A cheque-book register, full screen.  A adds a record through a
cap     form -- item number, date as YY/MM/DD, a type code, description,
cap     account and amount, with cheques entered as negative amounts --
cap     and C accepts it.  R lists every record with the balance, as
cap     shown; V edits, F changes the data file, Q quits.  Records go
cap     in testfile.dat in the current directory.  It needs TERM set.
try     checkfile
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/MTHB > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/MTHB
wait    2
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/MTHB
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     checkfile
wait    5
send    A
wait    3
send    101\r
wait    2
send    26/09/01\r
wait    2
send    CHK\r
wait    2
send    Electric bill\r
wait    2
send    UTIL\r
wait    2
send    -84.50\r
wait    3
send    C
wait    3
send    Y
wait    3
send    102\r
wait    2
send    26/09/03\r
wait    2
send    DEP\r
wait    2
send    Pay cheque\r
wait    2
send    INC\r
wait    2
send    1250.00\r
wait    3
send    C
wait    3
send    N
wait    3
send    R
wait    5
snap
send    \r
wait    3
quit    Q

shot    oleo
size    20 80
cap     GNU Oleo 1.6, a spreadsheet.  It opens on its copyright notice and
cap     the help character, and waits for a key before drawing the grid.  It
cap     used to stop on an illegal instruction before reaching this far; two
cap     fixes in the emulator's trap handling on 2026-09-11 let it run.
try     oleo
run     oleo
wait    6

shot    scqref
cap     The quick-reference card for sc, the spreadsheet: a document
cap     that prints itself, 360 lines in sections A to Q -- options,
cap     cursor movement, cell entry, files, ranges, the function
cap     lists.  Page it with less: space for the next page, q to leave.
try     scqref | less
os9     scqref ! less
run     scqref | less
wait    10
snap
send    q
wait    2

shot    factor
size    24 80
cap     factor prints the prime factors of each number it is given.
try     factor 1234567890 1000001 97
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     factor 1234567890 1000001 97
wait    5

shot    primes
size    30 80
cap     primes lists the primes between two numbers.
try     primes 1 100
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     primes 1 100
wait    5

shot    number
size    24 80
cap     number writes a number out in English words, a part at a time.
try     number 1234567
run     builtin cd /dd
wait    1
run     clear
wait    1
run     number 1234567
wait    5

shot    bc
size    16 80
cap     An arbitrary-precision calculator: the numbers are as long as they
cap     need to be, and `scale' says how many decimal places to keep.  -l
cap     loads the maths library, where a(1) is arctangent -- so a(1)*4 is
cap     pi, to whatever scale was asked for.
try     bc powers.b; bc seventh.b; bc -l pi.b
run     rm -rf /dd/tmp/BCC > /nil 2>/nil; mkdir -p /dd/tmp/BCC
run     printf "2^200\rquit\r" > /dd/tmp/BCC/powers.b
run     printf "scale=40\r1/7\rquit\r" > /dd/tmp/BCC/seventh.b
run     printf "scale=20\ra(1)*4\rquit\r" > /dd/tmp/BCC/pi.b
run     builtin cd /dd/tmp/BCC
wait    2
run     clear
wait    1
run     bc powers.b
wait    4
run     bc seventh.b
wait    4
run     bc -l pi.b
wait    5
